
aifoundry2 - AI Foundry lab machine with 1 ET-SoC-1 card (driver et_soc1 0.20.0)
  card 0  PCI 0000:02:00.0  /dev/et0_mgmt /dev/et0_ops  firmware 1.3.1 (as of 2026-09-25)
  The card runs its firmware's own clock/power governor (TDP 65 W); it reaches 800 MHz only from a cool start,
  and in this chassis its die rarely cools below 65 C, so it usually runs at 600 MHz.

  OUT OF SERVICE since 2026-10-07 16:46: even with every fan at full speed in the BIOS, the cooling at the card
  got worse, and idle it heated until it dropped off the PCIe bus (138 C, as on 2 Oct; nothing on the card stops
  it). Run nothing on the card until the lab says it is fixed, even when it is back on the bus (after a reset it
  looks healthy for about an hour while it heats). The machine itself is up; new users set up on aifoundry3 or
  aifoundry1. (In service 6-7 Oct; out of service 2-6 Oct.)

* Each device node can be open in ONE process at a time. Who is using the card: et-who (in scripts: et-who --check)
* Card use is logged (who, which program, when; never arguments or files). Last 24 hours: et-usage
* New here? Set up on aifoundry3 or aifoundry1 (et-lab-start there), not on this machine;
  the prompt for your coding agent: https://spacesheep.dev/@yaroslavvb/aifoundry-lab-start
* Reserve the card for a run (when it is back in service):  flock /run/lock/etsoc-shire0.lock <command>
* These commands change the card for every user: reset, trace level, telemetry stats reset (ettelem --reset-ms),
  temperature thresholds, TDP, frequency, active power management.
* Stop tools and samplers with Ctrl-C or plain kill, never kill -9: a request cut off mid-way leaves a
  stale reply, and the next program that opens the card dies with std::bad_function_call. To clear it once:
  /opt/et/bin/dev_mngt_service -m DM_CMD_GET_MODULE_POWER -n 0 -u 5000
* Vendor tools: /opt/et/bin (stock et-platform 353f20e build; on PATH in login shells).
  Link /opt/et/lib with -Wl,-rpath,/opt/et/lib.
* Host programs: register the runtime's log levels first in main (a race in libetrt crashes about one launch
  in 100 at 1.08 s otherwise): see registerRuntimeLogLevels() in github.com/yaroslavvb/et-soc1-prototyping.
* A GitHub Actions runner (aifoundry-org/hf-hackathon, runs as root) can use the card for CI jobs.
* tmux on this machine is a snap: /snap/bin/tmux (3.7c).
* /tmp is cleared at every boot: keep your work in your home directory.
* Crash dumps of your programs: coredumpctl list ; coredumpctl gdb <pid>
* Machine facts to record with a measurement: et-lab-manifest.  Health check (read-only): et-lab-health
* Lab accounts and notes: https://spacesheep.dev/@yaroslavvb/aifoundry-lab-accounts
